WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.430
It's a truth universally acknowledged that
with great power comes great responsibility.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.580
Taking into account both the comics and the
TV series, here are some of the worst things

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.910
the Umbrella Academy has ever done.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.790
Whether you're talking about the comics or
the TV series, there's no denying Reginald

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:17.450
Hargreeves is the worst.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:23.130
After all, he probably caused all those spontaneous,
non-consensual pregnancies through his magic

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.910
alien sparkles, resulting in the births of
the Hargreeves children.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:32.230
And then he raised the seven kids he was able
to purchase in a sterile, unloving environment,

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:37.560
where his only focus was on the children's
special abilities and not their emotional needs.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:42.231
Yet, while Hargreeves mistreated all of his
adopted children to varying degrees, it's

00:00:42.231 --> 00:00:48.050
no contest when it comes to which one of the
Hargreeves siblings drew the shortest straw.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:53.600
Poor Vanya Hargreeves was raised to believe
she had no powers at all and was, therefore,

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.760
unworthy in every sense of the word.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.260
It's only once she becomes an adult that she
learns she's actually the most powerful of

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:05.220
the seven, and that her father had been suppressing
her abilities with medication her whole life,

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.180
claiming she needed it to regulate her mental
health.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:13.210
Not only did he lie to Vanya for decades,
but he excluded her from the Umbrella Academy's

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:17.570
missions, causing her to feel distanced from
her family and doubt her own self-worth.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:20.740
"I'm afraid there's just nothing special about
you."

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:25.340
Reginald Hargreeves was a horrible father
no matter how you slice it, but his treatment

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.240
of Vanya took the cake.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.090
As a child, Allison Hargreeves received the
superhero moniker "The Rumor," thanks to her

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:36.130
ability to use her voice to convince anyone
to do anything she wanted.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:40.490
With a gift like that, the world is truly
Allison's oyster, and once she leaves home

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.680
as a teenager, she uses it to her full advantage.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.740
Allison rumors herself into fame and fortune,
which, while definitely not the most forthright

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:52.780
way to engage with the world, at least doesn't
seem to be hurting anyone.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.979
However, she crosses a line in both the comics
and the series when it's implied that she

00:01:56.979 --> 00:02:02.360
rumors a man named Patrick into falling in
love with her and, subsequently, marrying her.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.610
While we never get a detailed look at Allison
and Patrick's relationship, it seems that,

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:11.530
at some point, Allison told Patrick about
her powers and agreed to stop using them.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:15.900
However, their marriage fell apart when he
caught her using her powers on their daughter, Claire.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:18.050
"Good luck on your next film.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:21.100
Hope it turns out better than your marriage."

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:27.290
"Dysfunctional" is honestly a pretty tame
way of describing the dynamic among the Hargreeves siblings.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:31.780
But at least the Netflix characters make slightly
more of an effort to establish meaningful

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:35.380
relationships with one another than the siblings
in the comics do.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:39.650
However, one place in which the TV heroes
really drop the ball is toward the end of

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:44.230
the first season of The Umbrella Academy,
when the men of the Hargreeves family utterly

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:48.040
disregard the opinions and suggestions of
their sisters.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.410
After Luther realizes how powerful Vanya is,
he betrays her trust when she returns home

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:58.310
to ask for help, choking her unconscious and
locking her in a soundproof bunker underneath the mansion.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:02.099
Vanya pleads with him to let her out, but
he refuses to listen to her.

00:03:02.099 --> 00:03:06.630
Allison, whose throat Vanya slit in a fit
of rage, also demands that Luther release

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.130
Vanya, but he won't listen to her, either.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:13.520
Later, after Vanya escapes and destroys the
Academy, Allison beseeches her brothers to

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.220
try to talk to Vanya, but this time all of
them ignore her and attempt to attack Vanya

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:20.789
instead, which does not go well.

00:03:20.789 --> 00:03:25.400
Not only does this strip both Vanya and Allison
of their agency, but with the men calling

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.370
all the shots, the women are literally robbed
of their voices.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:34.530
In both the comics and the TV show, Klaus,
also known as The Séance and Number Four,

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:38.560
winds up time traveling back to the '60s,
where he enlists in the army and fights in

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:39.849
the Vietnam War.

00:03:39.849 --> 00:03:44.151
However, the details of Klaus' time in the
'60s are very different, and while the TV

00:03:44.151 --> 00:03:48.680
series sees him falling in love with, and
then witnessing the death of, a young soldier

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:52.200
named Dave, the comics don't give him a tragic
love story.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:57.840
Instead, they give him a job running a nightclub
and creating a Televator, a sort of TARDIS-like

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.750
transport originally invented by Reginald
Hargreeves that allows occupants to travel

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.239
through space, time, and other dimensions.

00:04:05.239 --> 00:04:10.209
When it's finally time for Klaus, Diego, and
Luther to head back to their time in the Televator,

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:15.000
Klaus hands an unexplained baby he'd been
carrying around off to a local Vietnamese woman.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:19.539
It turns out that, much to everyone's surprise,
the baby belongs to Klaus, although we never

00:04:19.539 --> 00:04:22.770
learn anything about the mother or even the
baby's name.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:27.169
Truthfully, it's probably good that Klaus
realized he had no business raising a baby.

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:30.961
On the other hand, especially given his own
upbringing, it seems rather callous that he

00:04:30.961 --> 00:04:36.080
just casually hands off his own child without
much of a thought before departing forever,

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:39.089
and he never mentions his offspring ever again.

00:04:39.089 --> 00:04:44.150
Both the second season of Netflix's The Umbrella
Academy and the second volume of the comics

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:49.240
center around the assassination of John F.
Kennedy, but the way it plays out in each

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.740
is very different.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:55.270
In both the TV series and the comics, Number
Five winds up duking it out with his older

00:04:55.270 --> 00:05:00.570
self after he's tasked with killing the president,
but in the TV show, it's actually the sinister

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:05.669
Majestic 12 organization that carries out
the assassination plot, absolving the Umbrella

00:05:05.669 --> 00:05:07.259
Academy of any role in the death.

00:05:07.259 --> 00:05:10.229
However, in the comics, things play out very
differently.

00:05:10.229 --> 00:05:14.939
After witnessing a cataclysmic future in which
Kennedy survives, setting off a chain of events

00:05:14.939 --> 00:05:19.900
leading to the end of the world, Five travels
to the past, intent on making sure the assassination

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:21.039
is carried out.

00:05:21.039 --> 00:05:25.989
Like in the show, his brothers are determined
to stop the assassination, but Five has accounted for this.

00:05:25.989 --> 00:05:29.979
Just when his siblings think they've stopped
the assassination, we see that Allison has

00:05:29.979 --> 00:05:35.719
taken Jackie Kennedy's place in Kennedy's
car, and she rumors Kennedy's head to explode.

00:05:35.719 --> 00:05:40.849
In Season 2 of the Netflix series, after the
entire Hargreeves clan accidentally travels

00:05:40.849 --> 00:05:45.659
through time back to the '60s, Klaus drops
into the year 1960 alongside the ghost of

00:05:45.659 --> 00:05:50.320
his dead brother, Ben, also known as The Horror,
and Number Six.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.370
Ben died when they were all teenagers, but
thanks to Klaus' ability to communicate with

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:58.979
the dead, Klaus has remained in constant communication
with him in the years since.

00:05:58.979 --> 00:06:03.080
After becoming irritated with Ben, however,
Klaus lies to their siblings after they've

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:07.680
reunited, telling them that ghosts can't time
travel when they ask about Ben.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.169
"You need serious help."

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:14.180
It's played for laughs in the show, but if
you think about it, this is a particularly

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:16.279
cruel thing for Klaus to do to Ben.

00:06:16.279 --> 00:06:22.069
Numerous times, Ben voices how much he misses
his siblings and wishes to be with them again.

00:06:22.069 --> 00:06:26.229
Even though the rest of their siblings express
a desire to speak to Ben, and Klaus knows

00:06:26.229 --> 00:06:30.169
he's Ben's only connection to the rest of
the world, he still refuses to acknowledge

00:06:30.169 --> 00:06:31.469
Ben's presence.

00:06:31.469 --> 00:06:37.189
Klaus is likely motivated much more by pettiness
than any sort of real maliciousness, but even

00:06:37.189 --> 00:06:41.020
so, punishing your dead brother by refusing
to let him communicate with his loved ones

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:44.809
just because you're annoyed is a pretty jerky
thing to do.

00:06:44.809 --> 00:06:49.439
In the Umbrella Academy comics, Klaus is more
of a sad character than a comedic one, but

00:06:49.439 --> 00:06:52.799
in the TV series, he largely serves to provide
some laughs.

00:06:52.799 --> 00:06:56.599
This is how it's framed when it's revealed
at the beginning of Season 2 that Klaus started

00:06:56.599 --> 00:06:58.400
a cult in the 1960s.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.199
While Klaus didn't set out to start a cult,
he quickly realized he could get special treatment

00:07:03.199 --> 00:07:08.219
if he used his knowledge of the future and
his powers to convince people that he was some sort of oracle.

00:07:08.219 --> 00:07:13.520
Klaus' cult, which he dubs Destiny's Children,
is played almost entirely for laughs, until

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.819
he eventually abandons them to return to the
future with his siblings.

00:07:16.819 --> 00:07:21.949
However, in the real world, cults are deeply
problematic organizations that thrive on brainwashing,

00:07:21.949 --> 00:07:23.729
manipulation, and abuse.

00:07:23.729 --> 00:07:28.050
Viewers know Klaus isn't a predatory person,
but he still wound up brainwashing his followers

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:30.190
through lies and manipulation.

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:35.309
After adopting his seven superpowered children
as babies, Reginald Hargreeves molds them

00:07:35.309 --> 00:07:37.639
into a crime-fighting superteam.

00:07:37.639 --> 00:07:41.729
But of course, defeated supervillains have
to go somewhere, and in the comics, it's revealed

00:07:41.729 --> 00:07:46.610
that after being subdued by the Umbrella Academy,
Reginald Hargreeves ships off the cowed baddies

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:48.279
to the Hotel Oblivion.

00:07:48.279 --> 00:07:52.529
The Hotel Oblivion isn't really a hotel at
all, but instead, it's a prison located in

00:07:52.529 --> 00:07:57.009
a hellish alternate dimension, where bad guys
are locked inside indefinitely.

00:07:57.009 --> 00:08:02.080
A glimpse inside one inmate's Hotel Oblivion
experience shows that the residents gradually

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:07.599
go mad from isolation, that the TVs play really
weird cartoons, and that the rooms are stocked

00:08:07.599 --> 00:08:10.670
with Hargreeves-authored self-help literature.

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:15.209
In the first season of the Netflix show, Vanya
spends much of the season vying for the competitive

00:08:15.209 --> 00:08:18.330
first chair violin position in her local orchestra.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:22.729
At the end of the season, she unleashes her
newly discovered powers and winds up accidentally

00:08:22.729 --> 00:08:26.830
blowing a chunk off the moon, triggering an
apocalyptic event.

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:31.819
However, in the comics, the orchestra Vanya
joins isn't just a regular musical group.

00:08:31.819 --> 00:08:37.330
Instead, it's an ensemble made up of, quote,
"madmen and murderesses," intent on bringing

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:38.960
about the end of the world.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:44.560
And they call themselves the Orchestra Verdammten,
which translates to "Orchestra of the Damned."

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.780
The conductor of the orchestra tries to recruit
Vanya early on in order to help them bring

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:51.140
about their goal, but she refuses.

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:55.400
However, once Vanya learns the truth of her
powers and her father's deception, she returns

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:59.370
to the Orchestra Verdammten and voluntarily
joins their ranks.

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:03.070
While it's understandable that Vanya would
be deeply hurt after learning that she's been

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:07.550
lied to and manipulated her entire life, joining
a group bent on destroying the world seems

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:08.910
like overkill.

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:13.440
This is one area where the Netflix show makes
Vanya much more sympathetic.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:17.670
Vanya's tour of destruction once she realizes
the extent of her family's betrayal doesn't

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:20.590
stop at her joining a villainous orchestra.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:25.450
Before she heads off to her fateful performance,
she also unleashes her powers on her family

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:29.660
mansion, and in the process, she kills her
family's primate caretaker, Pogo.

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:35.400
Throughout the lives of the Hargreeves siblings,
Pogo has served as both a mentor and a surrogate father figure.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:40.570
Vanya feels understandably betrayed when she
learns that Pogo was in on Reginald's secret,

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:45.040
but it still seems like a bridge too far when
she kills him in cold blood.

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:49.240
In the comics, she uses her powers to make
his head explode, while in the Netflix series,

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:53.180
she impales him on a pair of antlers mounted
above their fireplace.

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:57.370
One of a handful of important characters created
just for the Netflix series is the cunning

00:09:57.370 --> 00:10:02.170
Handler, who works for the Temps Commission
and serves as both a frequent ally and nemesis

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:03.600
of Number Five.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:07.540
While the Temps Commision exists to maintain
and protect the timeline, the Handler's role

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:11.900
has always felt a little more ambiguous, and
she always seems to have a hidden agenda that

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:13.370
serves only herself.

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:18.290
So it's worrisome when, as a tradeoff for
giving Five a means to return himself and

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:22.990
his siblings to their proper time after getting
stuck in the '60s, she requests that he assassinate

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:27.220
the entire Board of Directors of the Commission,
putting her in charge.

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:31.210
Granted, Five is rather desperate at the time,
but that doesn't change the fact that he's

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:36.930
willing to hand over control of time itself
to someone entirely untrustworthy and self-serving,

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:40.650
all in the name of getting himself and his
siblings back home.

00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:45.500
No list of terrible actions committed at the
hands of the Hargreeves family would be complete

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:51.140
without mentioning the, not one, but two apocalypses
the siblings have managed to cause.

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:55.890
The first one occurs when Vanya embraces her
powers as the White Violin and blows a chunk

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:59.030
off the moon that then comes hurtling toward
Earth.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:03.230
That apocalypse is thwarted in the comics
by Séance and circumvented in the show by

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:06.670
Five transporting all of his siblings back
in time.

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:12.250
But even so, there's no denying that the actions
of the Umbrella Academy caused it in the first place.

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:15.460
"Did dad say anything about the apocalypse
when you spoke to him?"

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:19.870
The second takes place in the '60s, after
the Academy successfully prevents the assassination

00:11:19.870 --> 00:11:25.000
of JFK, which winds up ultimately triggering
a nuclear war with the U.S.S.R.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:29.870
Once again, it's the Hargreeves siblings interfering
in the timeline that triggers the eventual

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:34.170
apocalypse, proving that in the world of The
Umbrella Academy, it's true that no good deed

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:35.680
goes unpunished.

